<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sart was a name used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for settled, town-dwelling and farming peoples, as distinct from nomads, especially in the oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. Originally meaning 'merchant', it came to cover Turkic- and Persian-speaking settled Muslims. Russian officials used it as an ethnic label, but the Soviet authorities abandoned it in the 1920s, and most Sarts were counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word is attested in 11th-century Turkic works, among them Mahmud al-Kashgari's Dīwān Lughāt al-Turk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-balasaguni">Yusuf Balasaguni</a>'s Kutadgu Bilig, meaning merchant or trader. It is traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> sārthavāha, caravan leader, through Iranian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> forms; John R. Perry takes the Iranian form to be probably Parthian sartvā. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> controlled the trade routes towards the nomads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and Turks called them Sarts, and the name spread to all settled Muslims; 13th-century Mongols used sartaq and sartaul for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> and Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, Sart became a synonym of Tajik, set against Turk for the nomads, and the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 16th century called the whole settled population they ruled Sarts, whatever its language. In the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>, according to Yuri Bregel, the Sarts descended from the ancient settled people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, lived mainly in the south and formed the tax-paying estate, while the Uzbeks were warriors; the Sarts used the name for themselves until 1917.</p><p>Russian officials met the term after taking the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. The 1867 statute of the Turkestan Governor-Generalship divided the native population into two nationalities, the settled Sarts and the nomadic Kirghiz, each with its own self-government, courts, land rules and taxes. Grammars of a Sart language were published, and the Turkestan Native Newspaper appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in Old Uzbek (Sart) and Old Kyrgyz. Scholars argued whether Sarts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, a separate people or simply townspeople; Leonid N. Sobolev wrote in 1874 that a Sart was not a separate tribe but an Uzbek or Tajik who lived in a city and traded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1920 Lenin asked for a map dividing Turkestan into Uzbekia, Kirghizia and Turkmenia, with no place for the Sarts, and in the 1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> enumerators were told to record people who called themselves Sarts as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>. The word survives as a derogatory term for Uzbeks, notably in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Since 1991 scholars of the new states have revived the question, some calling the Sarts Tajiks, others a distinct people or a social rather than an ethnic group.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khanate ruled Uzbek tribes, Turkmen and settled <b>Sarts</b> along the irrigated lower Amu Darya.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-khiva">Khanate of Khiva</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivalry between settled <b>Sarts</b> and nomadic Kipchaks caused civil wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kokand">Khanate of Kokand</a></li></ul></section>
